1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:02,600
Hotel rooms and school wings sit unused for days.

2
00:00:02,600 --> 00:00:05,000
Before they're used again, their pipes get flushed.

3
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:08,800
But the record only says a timer ran out.

4
00:00:08,800 --> 00:00:14,800
It never shows whether the stagnant water
in that branch was actually replaced.

5
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:18,000
This is our physical prototype.

6
00:00:18,000 --> 00:00:21,500
A flow sensor counts every litre into the branch.

7
00:00:21,500 --> 00:00:26,500
In our digital twin, a probe at the end of the pipe
shows when fresh water arrives,

8
00:00:26,500 --> 00:00:31,000
and the controller decides:
Verified, Failed, or Uncertain.

9
00:00:31,000 --> 00:00:34,800
It never passes a flush it can't prove.

10
00:00:35,000 --> 00:00:38,500
Across 520 simulated flushes,

11
00:00:38,500 --> 00:00:43,000
a fixed timer passed stale water 37% of the time,
a flow meter 31%. VeriFlush: zero.

12
00:00:43,000 --> 00:00:47,800
When a sensor failed, it said Uncertain
instead of guessing.

13
00:00:48,000 --> 00:00:51,500
Dubai already requires weekly flushing of empty hotel rooms.

14
00:00:51,500 --> 00:00:55,800
VeriFlush turns that log into evidence, supporting SDG 6.4.
Next: a pilot with a facility-management partner.

15
00:00:56,000 --> 00:00:59,500
Team Gurt. Making every flush count.
